# An EXTENSION ALLOW-LIST, not a deny-list. Ignore everything, then name what may
# be committed. This is what keeps the sample-keyed tables off a PUBLIC remote:
# there is no *.tsv rule below, and there must never be one.
*

# Recurse into every directory so the rules below can match inside them.
!*/

# THIS FILE ITSELF. Without this line `*` ignores .gitignore, the rules never
# reach the remote, and a fresh clone has no allow-list at all -- so the next
# `git add .` there commits every per-sample .tsv in the tree. It was untracked
# until this was noticed.
!.gitignore

# Nextflow work directories, whatever they contain.
**/work/**

# Explicitly allowed extensions, matched recursively.
!**/*.nf
!**/*.config
!**/*.py
!**/*.r
!**/*.R
!**/*.ipynb
!**/*.json
!**/*.sh
!**/*.sbatch
!**/*.dio
!**/*.png
!**/*.pdf
!**/*.md

# Delivery reports. SCOPED BY PATH, NOT BY EXTENSION, and deliberately.
#
# A blanket `!**/*.html` would also have committed results/_run_info/report.html
# and timeline.html, which name every task and therefore every sample: measured,
# 1,796 distinct sample ids in each. On a public remote that is the whole cohort
# roster. Only the rendered report is allowed, and only from its own directory.
!**/report/*.qmd
!**/report/_output/*.html
# The project file and the stylesheet are what make the .qmd buildable. Without
# them a clone has the source of the report and no way to render it -- which is
# the same failure mode as .gitignore not tracking itself.
!**/report/_quarto.yml
!**/report/*.css
!**/report/*.lua
# The left rail and its search, included after the body by _quarto.yml.
# Named exactly, not as **/report/*.html, so that no future .html dropped
# into a report/ directory is committed by accident.
!**/report/sidebar.html

# Component configuration written in YAML: the curated seed and the gene themes
# of integration/cteph_knowledge_graph. Scoped to config/ directories, so no
# other YAML is picked up by accident.
!**/config/*.yaml

# Never committed, whatever the extension (these rules come last, so they win):
# - raw API responses and language-model answer caches: large, regenerable from
#   the pipelines, and not meant for review;
**/raw/**
**/00.raw/**
**/cache/**
# - tmp/: scratch analyses and slide decks. Some of their files hold per-sample
#   values next to sample ids (carrier read counts, QC outliers), which must not
#   reach a public remote. Files already tracked there stay tracked.
tmp/**
